nL163: \nL164: Download raw file\nL165: \nL166: Outline\nL167: \nL168: Edit and raw actions\nL169: \nL170: # ZKML Inference Proving\nL171: \nL172: WARNING: This codebase is not audited and not production ready and is provided as is. Use at your own risk.\nL173: ## Overview\nL174: \nL175: DeepProve is a framework for proving inference of neural networks using cryptographic techniques based on sumchecks and logup GKR. Proving time is sublinear in the size of the model, providing order-of-magnitude speedups compared to other inference proving frameworks.\nL176: First-class LLM support: DeepProve proves end-to-end inference for transformer-based LLMs including GPT-2, Gemma 3, and Llama 2, covering all transformer layer types from embeddings through to next-token argmax. MLP and CNN inference is also supported.\nL177: \nL178: The framework quantizes weights and activations into a fixed zero-centered range. The default range is `[-2^11, 2^11-1]` (12-bit symmetric quantization); the bit width can be set via the `ZKML_BIT_LEN` environment variable (typical values: 8, 10, 12, 16).\nL179: ## How DeepProve Works\nL180: \nL181: DeepProve is built on a few simple ideas. If you skim only one section of this document, this is the one.\nL182: ### 1. Certify, don't re-execute\nL183: \nL184: A naïve ZK system would re-run each token's forward pass inside a circuit. For a sequence of length `t` and a per-token circuit of size `|C|`, that gives `O(t² · |C|)` prover work — unworkable on real-world LLMs.\nL185: DeepProve instead concatenates the input tokens and the claimed output tokens into a single sequence, and runs the model on that sequence once. Because of the causal attention mask and the deterministic argmax at the output, the model's prediction at position `t` certifies the token at position `t+1`. We prove a single forward pass; the structure of attention does the rest.\nL186: ### 2. Pre-process the model once\nL187: \nL188: A model file in SafeTensors, GGUF, or ONNX format is parsed into an operator graph, quantized into integer-only arithmetic (post-training static quantization, calibrated on a representative dataset), and committed to with a polynomial commitment scheme. The result is a reusable proving context (`*.pk` / `*.vk`) that amortizes across every subsequent inference proof.\nL189: ### 3. Prove layer-by-layer, then chain\nL190: \nL191: For every layer in the graph we run a specialised protocol:\nL192: \nL193:   * Linear ops (MatMul, Conv, RMSNorm, LayerNorm) — efficient sum-check protocols\nL194:   * Non-linear ops (Softmax, GeLU, requantization) — LogUp-GKR lookup arguments\nL195: \nL196: Per-layer claims chain back-to-front in a GKR-style composition, producing a single end-to-end proof.\nL197: The polynomial commitment scheme used in the public binaries is HyperKZG, built on top of 【56†arkworks†arkworks.rs】 and therefore generic over any pairing-friendly curve. The CPU build defaults to BN254; the GPU build (`--features cuda`) is currently BN254-only.\nL198: ## Documentation\nL199: \nL200: Deeper technical write-ups — proof protocols for each layer (embeddings, QKV, multi-head attention, positional encoding, LayerNorm, RMSNorm, argmax), softmax, EinSum, ReLU, MaxPool, range checks, lookup tables, and commitment schemes — live in 【57†`docs/`】 and are built with 【58†mdBook】 + 【59†mdbook-katex】:\nL201: \nL202:     cargo install mdbook\nL203:     cargo install mdbook-katex\nL204:     mdbook build docs --open\nL205: \nL206: ## Supported Models & Layers\nL207: ### Transformer (LLM)\nL208: \nL209: The recommended path is SafeTensors via Hugging Face: pass `--hf <model-id>` and `bench-llm` will fetch and cache the weights automatically on first run. GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX models are also supported.\nL210: Model  | Recommended loader  | Notes\nL211: --- | --- | ---\nL212: GPT-2  | `--hf openai-community/gpt2`  | SafeTensors auto-downloaded from HF. GGUF also supported (`--gguf gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`)\nL213: Gemma 3  | `--hf google/gemma-3-1b-it`  | SafeTensors only\nL214: Llama 2  | `--hf meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf`  | SafeTensors only. `--accuracy` mode currently falls back to default quantization for Llama 2\nL215: \nL216: Supported transformer layers:\nL217:   * Embeddings — provable token vocabulary lookup\nL218:   * Positional encoding — Absolute and Rotary (RoPE)\nL219:   * Attention mask — causal masking for auto-regressive generation\nL220:   * EinSum / Multi-head attention — batched matrix multiplication for QK^T and OV projections\nL221:   * Softmax — with provable range checks\nL222:   * LayerNorm / RMSNorm — vector normalization with proofs\nL223:   * Argmax / Logits — next-token prediction output layer\nL224: ### Traditional (MLP / CNN)\nL225: \nL226:   * Dense — fully connected layers\nL227:   * Conv2D — convolutions with FFT-based optimization\nL228:   * Activations — ReLU, GELU\nL229:   * Pooling — MaxPool\nL230:   * Reshape / Flatten / Add — tensor manipulation and residual connections\nL231: \nL232: ## Installation\nL233: \nL234: ### Prerequisites\nL235: \nL236:   * Rust + Cargo (latest stable)\nL237:   * Python 3.10+ — only required if you plan to run the MLP/CNN benchmark harness `bench.py`\nL238:   * CUDA 12.x toolkit + NVIDIA driver — only required for the GPU build\nL239: ### Build (CPU)\nL240: \nL241:     curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh\nL242:     source $HOME/.cargo/env\nL243:     cargo build --release -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL244: ### Build (GPU)\nL245: \nL246: DeepProve ships two GPU feature flags:\nL247: Feature  | Platform  | Effect\nL248: --- | --- | ---\nL249: `cuda`  | Linux + NVIDIA  | Replaces the CPU PCS with `HyperKZGGpu` and runs inference on burn's CUDA backend — fastest path\nL250: `wgpu`  | Linux / macOS / Windows  | Cross-platform GPU inference; PCS still runs on CPU\nL251: \nL252:     # Linux + NVIDIA: full GPU acceleration (inference + PCS)\nL253:     cargo build --release --features cuda -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL254: \nL255:     # Cross-platform GPU inference only\nL256:     cargo build --release --features wgpu -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL257: The default (no `--features` flag) is a CPU-only build using the `ndarray` backend.\nL258: ## Quickstart: Prove GPT-2 Inference End-to-End\nL259: \nL260: This walkthrough builds the binary, fetches GPT-2 from Hugging Face, and produces an end-to-end inference proof.\nL261: \nL262: ### 1. Build\nL263: \nL264:     cargo build --release -p zkml --bin bench-llm\nL265: ### 2. Get a GPT-2 model\nL266: \nL267: The recommended path is `--hf openai-community/gpt2`: `bench-llm` will fetch the SafeTensors weights and tokenizer from Hugging Face on first run and cache them locally for subsequent runs — no manual download required. SafeTensors is the most widely-used checkpoint format for transformer models, which is why we lead with it.\nL268: GGUF (`--gguf <path|url>`) and ONNX checkpoints are also supported; the GGUF path is convenient if you already have a pre-quantized file (e.g. `gpt2.Q2_K.gguf`).\nL269: ### 3. Prove a single sequence\nL270: \nL271:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL272:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64\nL273: \nL274: This builds the proving context, runs inference on a 64-token random prompt, generates a proof, and verifies it. Two CSV files are produced (see Reading the output below).\nL275: ### 4. Sweep multiple sequence lengths\nL276: \nL277:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL278:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64,128,256,512\nL279: \nL280: Each sequence length appends one row to the bench CSV.\nL281: \nL282: ### 5. Run on GPU\nL283: \nL284: Linux + NVIDIA:\nL285: \nL286:     cargo run --release --features cuda --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL287:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64\nL288: \nL289: The `cuda` feature switches the PCS to `HyperKZGGpu` and runs inference on the GPU backend.\nL290: ### 6. Cache the proving context\nL291: \nL292: The proving context (`pk` / `vk`) is the most expensive one-off step. Save it to disk once and reuse it across runs:\nL293: \nL294:     # First run: generate and save\nL295:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL296:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 \\\nL297:       --save_params setup\nL298: \nL299:     # Subsequent runs: skip context generation\nL300:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL301:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 \\\nL302:       --load_params setup\nL303: This writes/reads `setup.pk` and `setup.vk` in the current directory.\nL304: ### 7. Higher-accuracy mode\nL305: \nL306:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL307:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 --accuracy\nL308: \nL309: `--accuracy` enables outlier smoothing and per-tensor activation tracking, improving cosine similarity / perplexity at the cost of additional proving time. Available for GPT-2 and Gemma 3.\nL310: ### 8. Profile distributed proving locally\nL311: \nL312:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL313:       --model gpt2 --hf openai-community/gpt2 --sequence 64 \\\nL314:       --distributed --num_chunks 4\nL315: \nL316: `--distributed` simulates the chunked distributed protocol on a single host (useful for measuring memory split across N workers without provisioning a cluster). Output goes to `bench_distributed.csv`.\nL317: ### 9. Read the output CSV\nL318: \nL319: `bench-llm` produces two CSV files in the current directory:\nL320: \nL321: File  | Source  | Contents\nL322: --- | --- | ---\nL323: `bench-llm.csv`  | `timed_core`  | One row per timed function call across the run — useful for per-method profiling\nL324: `bench.csv` (or `bench_distributed.csv` with `--distributed`)  | `measure`  | One row per benchmark trial with aggregated metrics\nL325: \nL326: Columns in the per-run CSV (`bench.csv`):\nL327: Column  | Meaning\nL328: --- | ---\nL329: `model_name`  | `gpt2` / `gemma3` / `llama2`\nL330: `num_threads`  | Rayon thread count for this run\nL331: `quantization_time`  | One-off cost of converting the model to integer arithmetic (ms)\nL332: `context_generation_time`  | One-off cost of generating PK/VK (ms; `0` when `--load_params` is used)\nL333: `max_context`  | Maximum context window for this trial (tokens)\nL334: `min_user_len`  | Prompt length (tokens)\nL335: `inference_time`  | Quantized inference wall-time (ms)\nL336: `prove_full`  | Total proving wall-time (ms)\nL337: `proof_size`  | Serialized proof size (bytes)\nL338: `prove_full_memory_peak`  | Peak RSS during proving (MiB)\nL339: `token/sec`  | Derived as `max_context / prove_full`\nL340: ### 10. Try Gemma 3 / Llama 2\nL341: \nL342:     # Gemma 3\nL343:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL344:       --model gemma3 --hf google/gemma-3-1b-it --sequence 128\nL345: \nL346:     # Llama 2\nL347:     cargo run --release --bin bench-llm -- \\\nL348:       --model llama2 --hf meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf --sequence 128\nL349: \nL350: Hugging Face access requires `huggingface-cli login` for gated models (e.g. Llama 2).\nL351: ### 11. Run the standalone code example\nL352: \nL353: For a self-contained reference run with prompt `\"The sky is\"`:\nL354: \nL355:     cargo run --release --example chunk_llm\nL356: \nL357: Run `bench-llm --help` for the full list of flags and options.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFasterNTT/README.md at master · nict-sfl/FasterNTT · GitHub (https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/blob/master/README.md)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/nict-sfl/FasterNTT/blob/master/README.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 197\nL0: Skip to content\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: Toggle navigation\nL4: \nL5: 【0† 】\nL6: \nL7: 【1† Sign in 】\nL8: \nL9:   Appearance settings\nL10: \nL11:   * Platfo